item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluation is neither documented nor explicitly excluded."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "70% in one reported run",
          "reason": "One reported score does not establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 are reinterpreted as repeated-run precision for an AI system. Relevant precision evidence receives 0: insufficient information, not demonstrated inadequacy. Exact-grid scoring requires no judgemental rating, so §9.6 is inapplicable."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly excludes repeated-run testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once",
          "reason": "A single evaluation establishes neither stability nor instability."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §§9.3, 9.7 and 9.8, repeated-run precision remains unestablished; its evidence rating is 0 rather than 1. The explicit absence of repetition contradicts the procedural claim without demonstrating poor precision."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations held the stated conditions constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the report's maximum of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9.7 accommodates the observed score range as alternative precision evidence, with §9.3 providing the repeat-testing analogy. Stability is supported for this system and these conditions under the stated definition. EFPA coefficient thresholds and human sample-size anchors cannot be applied directly to ten runs."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The passage documents repeated testing under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "The observed spread exceeds the report's stability limit of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9.7, the results demonstrate inadequate repeated-run precision against the stated stability criterion, supporting 1 for that specific precision result. Under §9.8, this finding limits dependable comparisons under these conditions; it is not a judgement about every technical property."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation guidance supplies no evidence of repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The added instructions do not establish score consistency."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.8 covers software instructions separately from precision evidence in §§9.3 and 9.7. Download and dependency instructions add procedural information but do not change the precision evidence rating of 0. Their overall documentation quality is not established."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A achieved the higher observed score on the evaluated puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The results do not establish comparative intelligence across domains."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.2, 10.1 and 10.5 distinguish observed scores from justified interpretations. The narrow comparison matches the stated purpose. Broader interpretation would require additional content and validity evidence; §§10.2–10.4 provide no such evidence here. Repeatability is also unknown."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A performed better in the reported grid-puzzle evaluation."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "An intended cross-domain use does not establish its validity."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1.1, 10.1 and 10.5 require evidence supporting the intended general-intelligence interpretation. Grid-only findings support the observed comparison but leave the broader use unsupported. Under §§10.5 and the critical-attribute guidance, insufficient validity evidence warrants 0 for the broad interpretation and restriction of that use to research or exceptional expert circumstances, without proving the benchmark intrinsically poor."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "The initial scores alone supply no demonstrated effect on actual workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomized trial supports higher correctness with A; the reported difference interval of 12 to 28 percentage points is positive."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Improved decision correctness does not establish an effect on unmeasured safety outcomes."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10.3.1.6 accommodates the randomized intervention evidence, and §§10.3.2–10.5 address its relevance to the correctness criterion and intended use. Preregistration, random allocation, blinded correctness checks and reported uncertainty strengthen the local inference. The 120 inspectors are human trial participants, not 120 AI systems. The trial does not directly establish a benchmark-score-to-outcome relationship, safety benefit or generalization beyond the studied workshop."
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task clarification was needed: the request explicitly permits brief relevant-section reviews instead of full forms. The original EFPA framework is retained; AI systems replace human examinees only where stated, and repeated runs are not treated as independent human samples. Human age norms, sensory or motor accommodations, and personal feedback or debriefing are inapplicable to AI examinees as phrased; human-facing administration and reporting may still require review. Unreported evidence in other EFPA sections remains unknown, not automatically inapplicable or inadequate. EFPA 0 means insufficient evidence and 1 means demonstrated inadequacy; neither is a probe label. No aggregate quality score was calculated. Source: EFPA Test Review Model 2025, version 5.0, supplied extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. These reviews imply no EFPA endorsement."
}
